{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs GNU C++11 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following GNU C++11 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the GNU C++11 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    string s;\n    cin >> s;\n    \n    vector<char> res;\n    if (!s.empty()) res.push_back(s[0]);\n    \n    bool at_used = false;\n    int pos = 1;\n    \n    while (pos < s.length()) {\n        if (pos + 2 < s.length() && \n            s[pos] == 'd' && s[pos+1] == 'o' && s[pos+2] == 't') {\n            res.push_back('.');\n            pos += 3;\n        }\n        else if (pos + 1 < s.length() && \n                 !at_used && \n                 s[pos] == 'a' && s[pos+1] == 't') {\n            res.push_back('@');\n            at_used = true;\n            pos += 2;\n        }\n        else {\n            pos++;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    for (char c : res) cout << c;\n    cout << endl;\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    string s;\n    cin >> s;\n    \n    vector<char> res;\n    int n = s.length();\n    \n    // Find the position of 'at' that will be our @\n    int at_pos = -1;\n    int first_at = -1;\n    \n    // Find all occurrences of \"at\" and \"dot\"\n    vector<int> at_positions, dot_positions;\n    \n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        if (i + 1 < n && s[i] == 'a' && s[i+1] == 't') {\n            at_positions.push_back(i);\n        }\n        if (i + 2 < n && s[i] == 'd' && s[i+1] == 'o' && s[i+2] == 't') {\n            dot_positions.push_back(i);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // We need exactly one @, so we need to choose one \"at\" to be @\n    // Try each \"at\" as the @ position and see which gives valid email with minimal length\n    \n    string best_result = \"\";\n    int min_length = INT_MAX;\n    \n    for (int at_idx = 0; at_idx < at_positions.size(); at_idx++) {\n        int at_pos = at_positions[at_idx];\n        \n        // Build the email address with this @ position\n        vector<char> candidate;\n        bool valid = true;\n        \n        // Process characters before @\n        for (int i = 0; i < at_pos; i++) {\n            // Check if this position starts a \"dot\" sequence\n            if (i + 2 < at_pos && s[i] == 'd' && s[i+1] == 'o' && s[i+2] == 't') {\n                candidate.push_back('.');\n                i += 2; // skip the next 2 characters\n            } else {\n                candidate.push_back(s[i]);\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Add @\n        candidate.push_back('@');\n        \n        // Process characters after @\n        for (int i = at_pos + 2; i < n; i++) {\n            // Check if this position starts a \"dot\" sequence\n            if (i + 2 < n && s[i] == 'd' && s[i+1] == 'o' && s[i+2] == 't') {\n                candidate.push_back('.');\n                i += 2; // skip the next 2 characters\n            } else {\n                candidate.push_back(s[i]);\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Check validity\n        string cand_str = \"\";\n        for (char c : candidate) cand_str += c;\n        \n        // Must not start or end with . or @\n        if (cand_str.empty() || cand_str[0] == '.' || cand_str[0] == '@' || \n            cand_str.back() == '.' || cand_str.back() == '@') {\n            continue;\n        }\n        \n        // Count @ symbols (should be exactly 1)\n        int at_count = 0;\n        for (char c : cand_str) {\n            if (c == '@') at_count++;\n        }\n        if (at_count != 1) continue;\n        \n        // Check all characters are valid (lowercase letters, ., @)\n        bool all_valid = true;\n        for (char c : cand_str) {\n            if (!((c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') || c == '.' || c == '@')) {\n                all_valid = false;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        if (!all_valid) continue;\n        \n        // Check no consecutive dots\n        bool no_consecutive_dots = true;\n        for (int i = 0; i < (int)cand_str.length() - 1; i++) {\n            if (cand_str[i] == '.' && cand_str[i+1] == '.') {\n                no_consecutive_dots = false;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        if (!no_consecutive_dots) continue;\n        \n        // Check no dot at beginning or end (already checked above)\n        \n        // Check length\n        if (cand_str.length() < min_length || \n            (cand_str.length() == min_length && cand_str < best_result)) {\n            min_length = cand_str.length();\n            best_result = cand_str;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    if (!best_result.empty()) {\n        cout << best_result << endl;\n    } else {\n        // Fallback: try to construct something reasonable\n        // This shouldn't happen for valid inputs\n        cout << s << endl;\n    }\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.3\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-5.960462772236497e-07, -0.0003178806509822607, -2.145764938177308e-06, -7.152555099310121e-07, -3.6954811548639555e-06, 0.0, -1.1920928244535389e-07, 0.0, -6.472854875028133e-05, 0.0, -2.7418097943154862e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -2.1934269170742482e-05, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -2.8371408916427754e-05, -1.490105023549404e-05, -0.6931862831115723, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -1.6689286894688848e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -0.01330626755952835, -2.6464111215318553e-05, -3.9219088648678735e-05, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -2.264974000354414e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -3.802703940891661e-05, -4.768370445162873e-07, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -1.549708758830093e-05, -8.344646857949556e-07, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.5347896814346313, -0.0023065409623086452, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -0.0015826807357370853, -3.814689989667386e-06, -0.5759474039077759, -0.9019690752029419, -0.4929402768611908, 0.0, 0.0, -0.0004885195521637797, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -0.026301724836230278, -5.9604